this is a production of cornell  university  the the nation these plants are very  rhythmic  okay so think of skunk cabbage of jack  in the pulpit  your experience with much smaller  versions of this this is you know if  georgia o'keeffe could have done this  she would have  but this plant wasn't as popular at that  during her heyday than  as it is now now it's as we were  discussing before it's a little bit of a  growth industry and  greenhouses and botanical gardens around  the world um  one of the things i've been telling my  colleagues  in my own field is that the the draw of  this plant is not  that other flowers do this the draw of  this plant is that it has a hold  on the human imagination okay it has  charisma it fills rooms we're going to  have thousands of people come see this  so the outreach opportunity is not that  crop plants do this  and honey bees like this the outreach  opportunity is that this  is a bigger than life example of what it  takes  for plants to reproduce okay huge i mean  paul can show you the quorum  this is a like literal investment in in  mass  of stored sun power you know in as  starch  and what it takes to marshal all that  into this unbelievable effort of  flowering  and the fact that it's gone after three  days is  is is is you know almost mythological  to me so what we're really interested in  and i try to talk to students about  is that the plant is almost behaving  okay most plant biology happens so  slowly  uh and and so under our noses that we  don't we think of them as passive  right but what this plant is doing is  very active  it's growing fast enough for you to see  it  okay you come back every day and you see  oh okay it's three inches taller  it's wider it's heavier it's marshaling  all this energy  when it blooms this has been called on  the literature d-day  you know because of the incredible  explosion  of heat and stink and and in the visual  display this this skirt the spathe  becomes a beautiful purple funnel  um william barthevolt in in in germany  has actually  modeled with engineers the idea that it  might actually be a convection  chimney okay since since this whole  plant is  strongly thermogenic it's it's heating  up to eight to ten degrees  centigrade above ambient why on earth is  it doing that why is it doing it from  sunset on d-day to midnight which is  when it happens and we have thermal  images from two years ago showing  beautifully in color coding how this  thing gets really hot  but if you look at the the spade around  it  and realize that that the active part of  the flower  biology is down here in this little  chamber of these tiny little florets  what it's doing is it's heating as it's  emitting scent  and the convection currents that would  be produced by that would actually carry  that scent  up into the forest canopy and away from  the forest floor  in a rain forest okay it's very stable  place rainforest understories don't have  a lot of wind  all right so a rafflesia flower in the  same  habitat you know in tropical asia  very large very stinky not as strongly  heated or thermogenic the odor there  would bleb off in the boundary layer  and diffuse along the forest floor where  beetles and  and flies that come to dead animals  would perceive it and fly to the flower  and there's lots of nice david  attenborough footage of what happens to  rafflesia flowers and borneo and sumatra  etc  what the the german study was so why  that was so interesting to us was the  idea that  this plant may have a different niche  growing in the same forest it might be  advertising to  carry on insects up at the canopy  rather than down on the forest floor  using physics  basically creating a chimney and so one  of these we've been interested in  is what's the schedule why are they  producing  their blast of heat and scent at dusk  on d-day why does that persist until  midnight  this has been a production of cornell  university  on the web at cornell.edu
 

